  "summary": "An SF Standard report found that local police departments, including SFPD, Fremont PD, and CHP, searched Oakland PD's Flock ALPR system over 200 times on behalf of federal agencies including the FBI and in at least one case for an 'ICE case,' potentially violating California law. OPD denies directly sharing data with ICE and says it requires partner agencies to comply with state law, while privacy advocates argue the safeguards are illusory as OPD seeks to expand its camera network.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Local police departments in California have searched OPD's surveillance system on behalf of federal agencies over 200 times, according to the story.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "In one instance, the California Highway Patrol logged a search of OPD's system, giving the reason as \"ICE case.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "California prohibits local law enforcement agencies from sharing with federal agencies any data collected by automated license plate readers.",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "We're giving the illusion of oversight and guardrails here",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    }
  ],
